# <%= current_page.data.title %>

To create a message queuing service in the Cloud Platform, follow the [example guidance](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform-terraform-sqs/tree/main/examples) in the module.

## Allowing an SNS topic to send messages to your queue

If you subscribe your queue to an SNS topic, you must add a queue policy that allows the topic to send messages to the queue. Without it, the subscription is created but no messages are delivered.

The policy must restrict access to your topic using an `aws:SourceArn` condition:

```hcl
data "aws_iam_policy_document" "sqs_queue_policy" {
  statement {
    effect    = "Allow"
    actions   = ["sqs:SendMessage"]
    resources = [module.sqs.sqs_arn]

    principals {
      type        = "AWS"
      identifiers = ["*"]
    }

    condition {
      test     = "ArnEquals"
      variable = "aws:SourceArn"
      values   = [module.sns_topic.topic_arn]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_sqs_queue_policy" "sqs_queue_policy" {
  queue_url = module.sqs.sqs_id
  policy    = data.aws_iam_policy_document.sqs_queue_policy.json
}
```

To allow more than one topic, add each topic ARN to `values`.

> **Important:** Do not remove the `aws:SourceArn` condition. The `"*"` principal allows anyone to send messages to your queue; the condition is what limits this to your topic. A policy without the condition allows any AWS account to send messages to your queue.

Do not use SNS topic ARNs as principals. They are not valid principals, and AWS rejects the policy when it is applied. `terraform plan` does not catch this error.

You do not need to grant your application access to the queue in the queue policy. Attach the module's `irsa_policy_arn` output to your [IRSA](/documentation/other-topics/accessing-aws-apis-and-resources-from-your-namespace.html) role instead.

For more information, see the module's [access policy guidance](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform-terraform-sqs#access-policy).
